%U A397903 3,2,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,2,3,2,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3
%N A397903 a(n) is the minimum number of terms in a representation of prime(n) as a sum of distinct primes, each of which is itself recursively representable as a sum of distinct primes. The recursion starts with 2 and 3. a(n)=0 if no such representation exists.
%C A397903 If a(n)=2, then prime(n) and prime(n)-2 are twin primes.
%C A397903 Except for prime(n)=13 (n=6), a(n)=2 iff prime(n) belongs to A006512, the sequence of larger members of twin-prime pairs: a(n) = 2 <=> prime(n) is in A006512, prime(n) <> 13.
%C A397903 The only term currently known with a(n)=4 is 17 = 2+3+5+7, and the only terms with a(n)=0 are currently 11, 13 and 23. Computation confirms this through n=10000 (prime(n) <= 104729).
%C A397903 So, based on the conjecture (see A124867) - "Every number n > 17 is the sum of 3 distinct prime numbers" - we can propose the following hypothesis:
%C A397903 Conjecture: for all n > 9, a(n) = 2 or 3. This conjecture has been verified up to 10000.
%e A397903 2 -> a(1) = 1.
%e A397903 3 -> a(2) = 1.
%e A397903 5 = 2+3 -> a(3) = 2.
%e A397903 7 = 2+5 -> a(4) = 2.
%e A397903 11 -> a(5) = 0: not decomposable into a sum of distinct primes.
%e A397903 13 -> a(6) = 0: 13 = 2+11, but 11 is not decomposable into a sum of distinct primes.
%e A397903 17 = 2+3+5+7 -> a(7) = 4.
%e A397903 19 = 2+17 -> a(8) = 2.
%e A397903 23 = 3 + 7 + 13 = 5 + 7 + 11 = 2 + 3 + 5 + 13 = 2 + 3 + 7 + 11 -> a(9) = 0 because every decomposition into distinct primes contains 11 or 13.
%Y A397903 Cf. A001097, A124867, A224534, A070215, A054860, A006512.
%K A397903 nonn,new
%O A397903 1,3
%A A397903 _Yves Debeuret_, Oct 09 2026
